import{_ as r,C as a,o as i,c as l,j as n,a as g,E as s,a2 as o}from"./chunks/framework.sc8Z_ZE3.js";const v=JSON.parse('{"title":"Operations","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"admin/operations.md","filePath":"admin/operations.md"}'),d={name:"admin/operations.md"};function h(p,t,u,c,m,w){const e=a("Badge");return i(),l("div",null,[t[0]||(t[0]=n("h1",{id:"operations",tabindex:"-1"},[g("Operations "),n("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#operations","aria-label":'Permalink to "Operations"'},"​")],-1)),s(e,{type:"built",text:"Built"}),t[1]||(t[1]=o('<p>Operations shows whether the platform is healthy, what runs now, what ran, what it cost, and what needs your attention.</p><p><strong>Operations</strong> is its own area in the left rail, with these pages:</p><ul><li><strong>Overview</strong></li><li><strong>Operate</strong>: <strong>Live</strong>, <strong>Costs</strong>, <strong>Health</strong>, <strong>Runs</strong></li><li><strong>Govern</strong>: <strong>Decisions</strong>, <strong>Audit log</strong></li><li><strong>Cost</strong>: <strong>Usage &amp; costs</strong></li></ul><p>The Overview, Health, Runs and Usage &amp; costs pages decide nothing. Each item links to the page that owns the fix.</p><h2 id="overview" tabindex="-1">Overview <a class="header-anchor" href="#overview" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Overview&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The <strong>Overview</strong> opens with four tiles:</p><ul><li><strong>Needs attention</strong>: the number of open items, broken down by kind.</li><li><strong>Health</strong>: failing, degraded, unknown and ok checks at a glance.</li><li><strong>Spend</strong> for the current month, in USD. If some tokens have no price, the tile says what share is unpriced, so the real figure is higher.</li><li><strong>Decisions · today</strong>: today&#39;s gate decisions by outcome.</li></ul><p>Below the tiles, <strong>Needs attention</strong> lists each item with a severity word, a one-line cause, its age and one button to the page that fixes it. Items include:</p><ul><li>failing health checks (a new connection failure gets 15 minutes&#39; grace before it shows)</li><li>dead letters: background actions that failed and wait for a retry</li><li>governance reviews that have waited more than 24 hours for a second reviewer</li><li>approvals waiting on idle sessions, and clarifications unanswered for more than a day</li><li>workflow runs that are paused or have waited more than a day</li><li>dropped decision records</li><li>an unpriced model in use, or a budget at 80 % or more</li></ul><p><strong>Health at a glance</strong> repeats the health groups in one strip. <strong>Refresh</strong> reloads the page.</p><h2 id="live" tabindex="-1">Live <a class="header-anchor" href="#live" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Live&quot;">​</a></h2>',11)),s(e,{type:"built",text:"Built"}),t[2]||(t[2]=o('<p><strong>Live</strong> lists every active run with its <strong>State</strong>, <strong>Step</strong>, <strong>Where</strong> it works, its <strong>Last sign of life</strong> and its cost. A run with several items opens into them. Below the runs, <strong>Devices</strong> and <strong>Seats</strong> show the devices and seats used in the last 24 hours.</p><p>You see the Workspace you have switched to. Platform admins can switch to <strong>All workspaces</strong>.</p><h2 id="costs" tabindex="-1">Costs <a class="header-anchor" href="#costs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Costs&quot;">​</a></h2>',3)),s(e,{type:"built",text:"Built"}),t[3]||(t[3]=o('<p><strong>Costs</strong> shows what the runs of the last <strong>7 days</strong>, <strong>30 days</strong> or <strong>90 days</strong> cost and what they delivered. <strong>Group by</strong> <strong>Workflow</strong>, <strong>Work item</strong>, <strong>Run</strong>, <strong>Person</strong> or <strong>Workspace</strong>. Each row shows the <strong>API cost</strong>, the <strong>Runs</strong>, how many were <strong>Delivered</strong> (completed), the <strong>Pull requests</strong> and the <strong>API cost per delivered run</strong>. A total row sums the table.</p><p>Calls made through a person&#39;s own seat cost the platform nothing. Their API price is shown in a column of its own and never added to the API cost.</p><h2 id="health" tabindex="-1">Health <a class="header-anchor" href="#health" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Health&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Health</strong> groups its checks:</p><ul><li><strong>Platform</strong>: the API, the database, the scheduler and the background workers.</li><li><strong>Connections</strong>: the state of each connection.</li><li><strong>Syncs &amp; intakes</strong>: Knowledge syncs.</li><li><strong>Providers (last 1 h)</strong>: model providers, based on the last hour of calls.</li><li><strong>Background jobs</strong>: dead letters, circuit breakers and device clients.</li></ul><p>Each row shows <strong>Component</strong>, <strong>Status</strong>, <strong>Since</strong>, <strong>Last check</strong> and <strong>Detail</strong>. Click a row to open where you fix it.</p><h2 id="runs" tabindex="-1">Runs <a class="header-anchor" href="#runs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Runs&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Runs</strong> lists runs from chat, the gateway, devices and workflows over the last <strong>1 h</strong>, <strong>24 h</strong> or <strong>7 d</strong>. You can filter by source and outcome and search by user, agent or workflow. Open a run to see its steps and tool calls, the decisions that stopped it, its cost by model, and the setup it ran under. A run&#39;s content is hidden until you choose <strong>Show content (audited)</strong>; viewing it is written to the audit log.</p><h2 id="usage-costs" tabindex="-1">Usage &amp; costs <a class="header-anchor" href="#usage-costs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Usage &amp; costs&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Usage &amp; costs</strong> shows spend for <strong>This month</strong>, <strong>7 days</strong> or <strong>30 days</strong>:</p><ul><li>tiles for <strong>Spend (priced)</strong>, <strong>Priced coverage</strong>, <strong>Tokens</strong> and <strong>Active users</strong></li><li><strong>Daily spend (priced usage, USD)</strong>, which you can show as a chart or a table</li><li><strong>By model</strong>, <strong>By Workspace</strong> and <strong>By user (top 5)</strong></li><li><strong>Limits &amp; budgets</strong> with <strong>Limit</strong>, <strong>Scope</strong>, <strong>Cap</strong> and <strong>Used</strong></li></ul><p>Calls made through a person&#39;s own Claude Code seat are marked <strong>seat · not billed</strong>. A model without a price is flagged <strong>no price</strong> and counts as $0 until you set its price.</p><h2 id="budgets" tabindex="-1">Budgets <a class="header-anchor" href="#budgets" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Budgets&quot;">​</a></h2>',13)),s(e,{type:"built",text:"Built"}),t[4]||(t[4]=o('<p>Set budgets in <strong>Admin › Models › Budgets</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Add budget</strong>, then choose the <strong>Scope</strong> (<strong>A Workspace</strong> or <strong>A person</strong>), the <strong>Period</strong> (day, week or month) and the <strong>Amount (USD)</strong>.</li><li><strong>Warn at (% of the amount)</strong> and <strong>Stop at (% of the amount)</strong> set where the budget warns and where it stops. They start at 80 and 100.</li><li><strong>Claude Code per person per day</strong> is a daily cap per person for Claude Code. Each Workspace sets it, and a platform baseline applies where a Workspace sets none.</li><li><strong>Workspace cost caps</strong> lets a Workspace set its own per-session and per-request caps instead of the platform value.</li><li><strong>Models without a price</strong> decides what happens to unpriced models while a cost cap is active: <strong>Run and warn</strong> (usage shows as unpriced) or <strong>Refuse the run</strong> until they have a price.</li></ul><h2 id="setup-guide" tabindex="-1">Setup guide <a class="header-anchor" href="#setup-guide" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Setup guide&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The <strong>Setup guide</strong> (in the profile menu) is a checklist of seven steps for a new installation:</p><ol><li><strong>Create a Workspace</strong></li><li><strong>Connect a system</strong></li><li><strong>Add a Knowledge source</strong></li><li><strong>Set up the models</strong></li><li><strong>Set up single sign-on</strong></li><li><strong>Give people access</strong></li><li><strong>Choose the agents</strong></li></ol><p>Each step shows as done, open or needs attention. You can <strong>Skip for now</strong> the recommended steps (single sign-on and access), and <strong>Resume</strong> them later.</p><h2 id="emergency-stop" tabindex="-1">Emergency stop <a class="header-anchor" href="#emergency-stop" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Emergency stop&quot;">​</a></h2>',7)),s(e,{type:"built",text:"Built"}),t[5]||(t[5]=o('<p>On <strong>Live</strong>, an admin can stop all machine work at once: <strong>Stop everything in</strong> a Workspace, or, for platform admins in the <strong>All workspaces</strong> view, <strong>Stop everything on the platform</strong>. Before you confirm, the panel lists the runs that pause and the device jobs that stop. A reason is required, and a platform stop needs an extra confirmation.</p><ul><li>Running runs pause and keep their work. You continue each one from its own page. Runs waiting for a person are left alone.</li><li>Device jobs stop at once.</li><li>New starts are held until an admin chooses <strong>Resume…</strong> with a reason.</li><li>Every stop is written to the audit log and listed under <strong>Emergency stops</strong> on Live.</li></ul><p>Two narrower stops also exist:</p><ul><li><strong>Device runs on this platform</strong>: the platform admin&#39;s kill switch for device runs. Turning it off stops every running device job within seconds.</li><li><strong>Emergency stop</strong> on a single run: admins only, with a required reason. It ends the run for good, stops its device job and writes an audit record.</li></ul><h2 id="limits-today" tabindex="-1">Limits today <a class="header-anchor" href="#limits-today" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Limits today&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Unpriced models count as $0, so spend can be higher than shown. The <strong>Priced coverage</strong> tile tells you how much is unpriced.</li><li><strong>Costs</strong> counts pull requests, not merged tickets.</li></ul><h2 id="related" tabindex="-1">Related <a class="header-anchor" href="#related" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Related&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="/docs/start/status">Status</a></li><li><a href="/docs/rules/policies">Policies</a></li></ul>',8))])}const y=r(d,[["render",h]]);export{v as __pageData,y as default};
